A||N||42||trigger||how reliable is your fiero||||1||
Z||000000||trigger||11-21-2000||05:51 PM||||i was just wondering how reliable everybody's fieros are.  my 88 coupe 4 cyl. auto. isn't the best, but i'm still working out some bugs.  anybody have tips on how to make them more reliable or specific tools to carry.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||11-21-2000||06:26 PM||||30,000 miles and no major problems on my '88 Formula. I've had higher mileage cars that were absolutely trouble free before. My Fiero seems to be a pretty high maintanance vehicle. Lots of little stuff to keep me busy.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-21-2000||07:38 PM||||Two breakdowns in 13+ years of ownership for my 87 GT.  Both were dead batteries. Almost everything is maintenance. Replace oil sending unit twice, tach filter, EGR valve (corroded), front brakes, (still have original rears), A/C repair ($800), distributor and ignition coil. New Borla exhaust and catalytic converter.<BR>My 87 has been quite reliable. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||jlruiz67||11-21-2000||07:43 PM||||I must say that my 87 Fiero has had a very good life due to preventive maintenance and schedule maintenance, I tend to believe that if the car was maintained properly it will take care of you. My Fiero has 210,000 miles on it and it doesn't eat any oil, it runs great the only problem is poor gas mileage of 17 miles per gallon, I have had 3 Fiero's the fist one was a 84 that burned the second was a basket case that I wanted to restore and a friend of mine still has in good condition now, the third I still have an probably will have forever. Different owners treat there Fiero differently and that is why some are real problems. I hope you don't shy away from this up to 13-year-old car because I think that any car this old is due to have some problems from age.<P>[This message has been edited by jlruiz67 (edited 12-04-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by jlruiz67 (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-21-2000||07:57 PM||||Mine runs just fine. needs some brake work is all.<P>on thing you want to replace on 87-88 4 cyl is the MAT sensor to the right of the TBI. These can get crapped up with age which can cause soem rather bizar things to happen. (They can also get cooked if the engine coughs into the intake.) You want the plastic cage type sensor, never the metal cage one. Borg Warner has the right one. It matters which one you use.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||11-21-2000||09:05 PM||||I bought my '84 SE in June '99.  First thing I did was replace a weak alternator and battery and thought everything would be ok.  Well, I was unaware that it was only the beginning.<P>Ok, DNF's in the past year:<P>1. Clutch went out month after I bought the car.<P>2. First highway test, week before the MI Fiero show, alternator belt exploded, literally.<P>3. Used the spare alternator belt that came with the car.  Must have been old or something because it wore quickly and came loose on the way to work next day.<P>4. Positive lead wire to battery meltdown.<P>5. Previous owner's household twist-tie connector came loose.  Lost power to fuel pump and many other things.<P>6. Swapped engine harness, solved my last two problems but forgot to loom the spark plug wires.  Hey you forget things working in the dark at 3:00am.  Spark plug wire caught fire on way home from work next day.  Opps, that one was my fault.<P>7. Starter bolt fell out on the way to work.  Was able to make it home.<P>I'd say most of these problems weren't all that bad.  Definetly the problems the previous owner left for me didn't help.<P>All I can say is it runs great now.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||sqoach||11-21-2000||09:14 PM||||i think i've put more money into my car (86 coupe) than i bought it for (paid $1,650 for it by the way) it seems that something is always going wrong with my car.  i guess i got a lemon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh||||reg||9
Z||000007||2xsess||11-21-2000||09:34 PM||||Well, I agree with the statement about previous owners. that is a major factor in the reliability of the car. When I got my car it was missing about 12" of the wiring going to the VSS and the O2 sensor wire has been cut and poorly spliced. I bought it in Daytona and drove it the 4 hours home with no problems though.Preventative maintenance is KEY!!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||strdream||11-21-2000||10:00 PM||||  My neighber gave me his theory on used car buying. It goes somthing like this.<BR>  If are buying a used car, get one that was owned by a woman.  He explained, She probably didn't try to make any repairs to it unless she knew what she was doing.  She took it to a shop and they recommened repairs she didn't even need but she said go ahead. She looked for a shop she could trust.  Qualities she looked for are Professionalizm, neatness, courtesy, honesty.  She spent more than a guy would have but she is selling a well maintained car.   <BR>  The next option is buy one from an accountant or doctor, lawyer or someone else that doesn't have a tool box.    ||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnnyK||11-21-2000||10:22 PM||||TIgger: What do you mean you forgot to loom the spark plugs? One of my wires is too short, so it's just laying over the valve cover. Is that what you mean?<BR>Old Lar: Maybe I misread, but did you say you have the original (13 year old) brakes on there??||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tigger||11-21-2000||10:41 PM||||No, the wires were all running over the valvecover because I didn't run them throught those plastic clip thingys.  My longest plug wire eventually jimmied itself to where it got too close to the exhaust manifold.  They were the old plug wires that came with the car and I was going to replace them anyhoo.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-21-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000011||overlord||11-21-2000||11:43 PM||||this is what my grandpa always said.<BR>"expect to shell out $500 a year for repairs."<P>if i average it out...thats about right for my 84 coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000012||007DOUG||11-22-2000||12:50 AM||||Very reliable, I've had a GT upto 205K (and still going for new owner), still have my 86 SE V6 with 153K. But I'm really anal about my maintenance. If you take care of your fiero, it'll take care of you. I've never been stranded. <P>I just took my blue GT to the fiero factory for a tranny swap: 1600 mile trip without a single problem.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Cheever2||11-22-2000||02:13 AM||||Bought my 88 Coupe 4-cyl last January.<BR>April - clutch<BR>May - plug wires<BR>June - oxygen sensor<BR>July - ignition module<BR>August - battery<BR>September - tires (no they weren't Firestones)<BR>October - radiator<BR>November - belt<P>Every time I get in it I wonder what's next.<BR>But I still love it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 11-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Archie||11-22-2000||07:55 AM||||I've owned Fieros for 15 years and have not been let down yet. Seeing some of the horror stories that we see everyday on these forums, I'm glad that all of my Fieros have been V-8 cars.<P>My current daily driver is an 87 (Finale) V-8 with the Isuzu 5 speed & I wouldn't be afraid to drive it anywhere anytime.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000015||82-T/A||11-22-2000||09:00 AM||||Not at all... I'm scared just to drive it down the street in fear that it will break down.<P>It went 120 thousand miles without a SINGLE problem.. nothing, broke down only once, and it was a bad Ignition Control Module... $14 dollars later, I was back on the road. <P><BR>But at one time my waterpump went out and the car seriously overheated when I Was on the highway. I took it into a shop and they said it was a bad head gasket. I didn't know it was hte waterpump at the time. So I did a valve job among other things, and now the car doesn't seem to have any power above 3,400 rpms. I did everything right, maybe I need new valve springs... I dunno..<P>it's depressing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Todd||||reg||1
Z||000016||DJRice||11-22-2000||09:33 AM||||I had to put a water pump on my 87 SE V6 last summer, but she got me home before she gave it up.  <P>Other than that, the only other stuff Ive done is a brake job and tune up.  Im up to 146K and she still runs strong. <P>My brothers 86 SE V6 has not been quite as dependable, but when we got it, it had been parked for quite a while and only had about 68K.  He has put about 25K on it in the past year and we have replaced the EGR Valve, TPS, 02 sensor (last weekend), fuel pump, brakes.  <P>Weve done the fuel pump on his car twice, but he ran it out of gas twice which kills the fuel pump.  I finally adjusted his gauge so that 1/4=1/4 instead of 1/4=E.<P>We have an 86 4cyl that has almost 190K in it and it runs pretty well but its time for a brake job on that one too.<P>  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000017||Raydar||11-22-2000||11:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DJRice:<BR><B>  ...I finally adjusted his gauge so that 1/4=1/4 instead of 1/4=E.<BR>  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah. Both of mine are like that. Have never run out, though. I wonder what changes to make this happen. <BR>Bad sender? Bad connections? <BR>Moving the pointer is probably the easiest fix, but I wonder if anybody else has actually figured out what caused it in the first place.<P>Raydar <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Raydar||11-22-2000||12:00 PM||||Oops. Forgot the main topic. <BR>Mine have never stranded me. Have put nearly a thousand worth of odds 'n' ends into the Formula, since I bought it. Would pretty much drive it anywhere. <BR>I don't drive the coupe much. Needs the  tensioner replaced. Don't want to get stranded if it throws the belt. Don't want to buy a tensioner either, since I'm gonna swap in a 2.8. <BR>Maybe I'll try a new fuel sender while I'm changing the pump.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000019||Old Lar||11-22-2000||12:11 PM||||JohnnyK.. Yup the rear brakes on my 87 Fiero are original.  I'm real easy on brakes with a manual transmission. May need a brake job this year.  I do mostly highway miles. My last daily driver (Suzuki Sidekick 4 door) had 75000 miles on it before the front brakes needed replacement.  It still had original rears when I traded it in after 7 years of ownership.<P>The Fiero only has 82000 on the odometer.  Just lots of easy driving, and frequent oil changes.||||reg||10
Z||000020||william_thorniii||11-22-2000||12:13 PM||||Friends,<P>I guess I am finally the lucky one.  My driver I purchased over 2 years ago.  I have put over 20k per year on her (Just turned 88K yesterday).  I can think of maybe 3 things I have had to replace.  Lets see: alternator (preventative @ 75K), valve cover gasket (x2 won't buy rubber again), belt tensioner... think that is it... Ooops, I forgot I replaced the headlight bushings, tires and muffler too. <P>Either way I feel very fortunate.  I do take decent care of her mechanically (shut up Drew and Scott).  I did purchase the car from its original owner... a middle aged lady.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Racer 1||11-22-2000||01:58 PM||||My 86 fiero coupe 4 cyld iron duck with 210,000 miles on her has been a real booger. 1 water pump<BR>3 alternators<BR>1 distributor assembly<BR>3 clutches<BR>4 starters<BR>5 batteries<BR>1 new brake master cylinder<BR>1 new throttle cable the oem one "Got melted on header"<P>Finnally upgraded to FORD starter siliniod to relieve hot weather no start ups.<P>Took out the crappy header and exhuast and put in HOOKER super comp header and custom made exhuast a glasspack for a muffler.<P>------------------<BR> <P>||||reg||9
Z||000022||fierospeeder||11-22-2000||03:16 PM||||84 fiero 96,0000 miles <P>replaced exhaust system, not including headers or the air cleaner heater portion<P>new oxygen sensor, egr valve, sparkplug wires, belt, tires, new tierod(my stupid mistake that got it bent and i wasn't driving), brakes, engine coolant sensor, new battery, new starter, new oil pump, solved the oil light coming on at idle.<P>I do have problems with it starting in the cold, i havn't tried yet with the new more Cold amps battery, i would hear the solenoid clicking, and i think the heat kills the solenoid also.<P>i still have my oil pan leak, i think its from blowby, i havn't done compression check yet, might be bad rings, lifters are little bit loud.  Not sure if the previous owners changed the oil as much as i do.  <P>Had no real problems except changing sensors ,and plug wires cause of the heat, only problem was my belt breaking and my car started to overheat, I did drive the car without the waterpump, the temp was more then 220 and the engine was fine when i got it to trak auto for a new belt, few miles away.  The coolant was overflowing and the oil was smoking from the oil fill cap. <P>Strong engine on it eventhough i race it a lot on the streets, and it still hasn't given me an "84 fiero fire" ||||reg||1
Z||000023||StuGood||11-23-2000||04:31 AM||||I'd brag on my Fieros, but as soon as I said something like "Yep, 14 years, and she still has the original cigarette lighter <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!," I'd hear this <B>"pop!"</B> (cigarette lighter exploding) and see a little thin column of smoke rising from the console  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.  By the way, I don't smoke.<P>Actually, I've had great luck with Fieros.  The 1986 SE's clutch let go after a couple years, but that's because somebody couldn't resist horsing around, giving it a hard life.  The replacement clutch has lasted 12 years, and it's ready to be replaced again (I feel relatively safe, bragging on something that is already on its last legs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).<P>Recommendations for preventative maintenance?I'm the wrong guy to ask, since I've been lucky.  Do the normal maintenance, read the service manual to familiarize yourself with the car, and you should be ready for anything.  Oh - and keep a spare ignition module in the car!   <BR>     ||||reg||12
Z||000024||Bob Englert||11-24-2000||11:48 PM||||I have owned my 85 SE 4cyl, 5sp for about 3 1/2 years.  I have put about 45K on it since I bought it (now has about 119K).  <P>I did a fair amount of maintenance on it when I bought it (front brakes, replaced drivers cooling pipe, rebuilt front suspension).  Since I drive it all over on business and I don't want to be left stranded, I had the Fiero Factory replace the phenolic timing gear with a metal gear a few months ago.  I do use Mobil1 5W-30 oil in all my Fieros.  <P>I try to stay on top of routine maint. and try to anticipate major potential problems like the timing gear.  I think my Fiero has been very reliable and I wouldn't hesitate to drive it anywhere.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000025||nivlek68||11-25-2000||03:44 AM||||no problems &lt;yet&gt; i have drove my 86 2m4 sc on from cleveland oh to ceder bluff va about 4 times. also to milton wv from cleveland oh 3 times,the tune up and oil changes help it on its merry way.||||reg||14
Z||000026||Haze_Performance||11-25-2000||04:01 AM||||I would drive my '85 <B>ANYWHERE</B>.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif">   <BR><B>"AHHHHH!!! All Fiero's don't start on fire you BONEHEAD!!"</B>||||reg||14
Z||000027||Boxdin||11-25-2000||10:54 AM||||I'm amazed just how dependable my 88 Formula is! Before knowing any better, I took a 1200 mile trip with old and poor functioning ignition and it still had decent power, milage, and reliability. That car now has 133k, and after sitting for months at a time in storage, it still fires up immediately and runs perfect. <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000028||William Federle||11-25-2000||11:08 AM||||I got my 1984 Fiero a year from this last August. Since then, I've been towed home three times and almost a 4th time:<BR>1. My battery cable to the starter burned in half after the insulation was melted by contacting hot components - it wasn't correctly routed or insulated. <BR>2. A fusible link fried down by the starter (it wasn't properly insulated and that is a very poor location for it).<BR>3. Item number 2 re-occurred. This time I relocated the fusible links to a cooler and more road-repairable location near the battery.<BR>4. My starter wouldn't turn over - the tow truck operator hit it with a metal towing hook while I tried to start it and unstuck it. I got a new AC delco starter and heat shield.<P>Now my biggest concern is excessive oil consumption. If I could just hit the lotto I'd drive this baby down to Barrington Ill. and get Archie to put a new V8 into it. One way or another, I'm hooked on this car and I'm going to keep it. I'm also going to keep my AAA membership.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||82-T/A||11-25-2000||02:04 PM||||I hear you William. I was one of the lucky ones that got to have a Fiero when the mileage was low. My first Fiero had 63k miles on it when I bought it. It had just had a massive tuneup and everything that needed replacing or should have been was replaced. I got it in 1997 with that.<P>I drove it for another 65+k miles.. in only 3 years...... ??? Dunno how I managed that.. but I never had ANY problems until the waterpump went out on the highway. The shop diagnosed it as a blown head gasket.. so.. I trusted them.. and I had it towed home, and I spent 3 weeks doing a valve job.. only to find out it was only a bad waterpump....<P>Anyway.. it runs weird now... valve lash is right.. but it has no power over 3,500 rpms.<P>???<P>Who knows.. I'm just about to drive my 1984 Fiero 2m4 SE to my parents house. I haven't driven it in over 6 months.. so hopefully all will go well.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE||||reg||1
Z||000030||KissMySSFiero||11-25-2000||02:47 PM||||I Drive a formula.  only got stranded once that i can remember.  Ignition module went out goin from orlando to tampa.  I made it halfway.  and i have had a clutch put in.  everything else was little things here and there.  I didnt really keep track of it all.  It seems if something breaks its because of my half a$$ work i did.  word of advise.  dont cut corners.  it always comes back to bite ya. I wouldnt hesitate to drive my car anywhere.  Im plannin a trip from Fl to Indiana.  then to Nebraska.  and then out to Washington State.  And back.  I got family all over.  but i wouldnt hesitate to make that trip.  its been done in my car before by the previous owner(my brother)  Hey maybe on the way back ill stop in Illinois so i can get a look at some of archies stuff.  Id love an LT1 in my car...||||reg||1
Z||000031||drednot||04-25-2001||07:19 PM||||so far, it's been the worst car that I have ever owned.  It is an 80's thing I think.||||reg||8
Z||000032||Wade87GT||04-25-2001||07:44 PM||||Let's compare, was married for 13 years, owned my 87 GT for just over 12 (bought the car 2 yrs after we married). Thinking back on all the horror stories I endured with my ex, my Fiero has been really good to me. At 152,000 miles and I can still catch 2nd gear, even with 245-50-16 tires on the back and burn rice all day long.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000033||Fierodave2001||04-25-2001||08:16 PM||||Well, lets see,  87GT V6  owned new since  09/86<P>Two oil changes<BR>Replaced Battery<BR>Replaced shrader valve/recharged AC just last month and ....<P>Gosh, that's all so far<P>Miles to date 1750.  <P>Wow, I see in all the earlier posts, I have alot to look forward to.   Oh boy.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Fierodave2001 (edited 04-25-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000034||Spektyr||04-25-2001||09:25 PM||||Well, I've only had my 85GT for a week now, but it's fired up everytime right off so far.<P>Already replaced the stock air filter with a K&N and knocked the stupid wall out of the driver's side intake tube.<P>Had a bit of a low oil pressure / high temp issue at the drive-thru today, but it's a tad low on oil (oil change tomorrow) and the new low temp thermostat and fan switch go in at the same time.  I shut it off before the temp pegged out and let the fan run for awhile to cool it off before I drove home.<P>I also have a bunch of new electricals going in tomorrow, Accel coil and HEI control module, Splitfire wires and plugs, and the Catalytic convert gets deep-sixed on Friday.<P>My attitude on engine repair is to find the weakest link, replace it before it fails and you greatly reduce breakdowns.  Also helps that I carry practically all my tools with me wherever I go.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000035||FieroLisa||04-25-2001||09:46 PM||||I had my 86 sport coupe with 104,000 miles on it- and she never ever gave me a major problem. I had to get a new clutch but then again I learned to drive a manual with her. She had all sorts of suspension issues that I guess I could have dealt with but I didn't care. Best of all, she started every time I turned the key for the entire time I had her.||||reg||1
Z||000036||ElricSI||04-25-2001||11:19 PM||||85 GT - 103 K (only about 3k in the last 4 years)<P>When I bought the car, it needed a huge amount of work (which I had done happily).  After I finally fixed everything it ran great for about 4 years.  Since then I have driven it very little.  I leave it sitting on the street for NY winters.  The car no likey that.  In the last year or so I have done an obscene amount of stuff to get it fully operational again.  (Heater core - twice I believe, new brakes - discs and all, new front shocks, yada yada.)<P>Moral of the story: drive your Fiero regularly or garage the sucker, plus winter is bad.<P>------------------<BR>Proud Owner of 85 GT||||reg||4
Z||000037||JSocha||04-25-2001||11:46 PM||||Would the temperature guage inside the vehicle that now acts more like a bad "compass" count?  Got lost this evening trying to follow it home as it was pointing what I thought was North and I only live a little over a mile from work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 04-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||Cameron||04-26-2001||01:46 AM||||My '87 GT runs great.  No problems with her.  Starts everytime I turn the key and no check engine light or stalling.  She needs a new clutch, but hell no complaints.  My previous lady was a '88 Formula and she was and still is in pristine health aside from needing some paint.<P><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000039||Cheever2||04-26-2001||02:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever2:<BR>Bought my 88 Coupe 4-cyl last January.<BR>April - clutch<BR>May - plug wires<BR>June - oxygen sensor<BR>July - ignition module<BR>August - battery<BR>September - tires (no they weren't Firestones)<BR>October - radiator<BR>November - belt<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Let's see, since then...<BR>Replaced heater core<BR>Replaced exhaust manifold<BR>Replaced radio<BR>Discovered reverse lights don't work.<BR>And now I have a backfire problem which could be a clogged cat.<BR>And the beat goes on...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000040||pontiacs4me||04-26-2001||02:37 AM||||Ive put 3500 miles on mine in the past 2 months.  Mostly city driving, delivering pizzas.  no major problems, I broke down at work one day, turned out to be a bad connection at the junction box by the fuse block.  And once it started pissing antifreeze everywhere, The thermostat housing neck, where the heater core line plugs into it, the brazing let loose, and it started spewing everywhere, so I ground all the braze off and welded it back together.  All fixed now. My car has 170,000 and the engine has 120,000.  I guess Ill find out how reliable it is in 11 days, when I drive to CA.||||reg||1
Z||000041||rogergarrison||04-26-2001||07:03 AM||||only problems Ive had in almost 4 yrs, coil gave up, but made it home barely. Ignition module went south, had to get towed. An alternator gave up at home in the driveway. Id get in and drive mine to Calif rite now without worrying. One thing that applies to all cars...never do any kind of work then leave for a long road trip. Do your tuneup, brakes, whatever a week or so ahead of time so you dont find out 500 miles from home you forgot to tighten something.||||reg||1
Z||000042||FieroHarry||04-26-2001||08:17 AM||||I got mine almost 4 years ago with 176K miles on it. Since then Ive racked it up to 262K miles.<BR>Im rather pleased with the amount of break downs ive had. Clutch/alternator/fuel pump.<BR>Nothing really drastic and not much in the way off stranding me except the time the fuel pump died.<BR>All in all this is problably the best used car Ive ever owned.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Harry<BR>FOCOSEVA<BR>1985 GT 262K+ "TEMPTATION"||||reg||12
